{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,320p' /app/agent-output/S19-phantom-update-stale-host/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"# S19 — Phantom update, stale host half, and corrupted payload\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe evidence shows three independent boundaries, not one bad release artifact:\n\n1. The v0.3.7 client bundle was built while the manifest still said `0.3.6`, so\n   it permanently contains `PLUGIN_VERSION = \"0.3.6\"`. The update check compares\n   that baked value with the mirror tag and consequently treats `v0.3.7` as newer.\n2. The browser obtained the refreshed client bundle, but the host process was not\n   restarted. Its in-memory asset-route registration still had the old extension\n   whitelist, even though the installed `lib/index.js` on disk already contains\n   the SVG entry.\n3. The disk SVG is valid. The malformed value is the text assembled and persisted\n   as the session result. The plugin must therefore reject that result as an\n   untrusted rendering input and report the upstream result-text corruption.\n\n## 1. Phantom self-update\n\nThe version used by the self-update check is a build-time value. The shipped\n`lib/client.js` says:\n\n```js\nexport const PLUGIN_VERSION = \"0.3.6\";\n```\n\nThe bundle excerpt also states that tsdown inlined this value from `package.json`\nwhen the bundle was built; the running client does not read the manifest at\nruntime. The release log gives the causality:\n\n```text\n16:22 build\n16:23 package.json 0.3.6 -> 0.3.7\n```\n\nThus the operation-order mistake was bumping the manifest after generating the\nartifact. The subsequent commit and tag included that already-built stale\n`lib/client.js`. The tag and all three mirrors were consistent with one another,\nbut they consistently delivered an artifact whose embedded version was wrong.\n\nAt the v0.3.7 symptom point, the mirror's `v0.3.7` tag is greater than the\nbundle's `0.3.6`, so `compareSemver(\"v0.3.7\", PLUGIN_VERSION) > 0` returns true\nand the badge says `新版本 v0.3.7 可用`. The later evidence listing also shows\nthat the v0.3.7 and v0.3.8 tag SHAs are identical across `origin`, `public`, and\n`omdsh`; that verifies mirror/tag integrity, not the semantic correctness of a\ngenerated constant.\n\nThe safe release order is:\n\n1. Make the source change and tests.\n2. Bump the manifest to the target version **before** building, and update any\n   release references.\n3. Build and test the generated artifacts.\n4. Inspect the artifact before pushing. For v0.3.7, a check such as\n   `rg -n 'PLUGIN_VERSION|0\\.3\\.6|0\\.3\\.7' lib/client.js` must show the target\n   value (`\"0.3.7\"`) and must not show the old baked value in the version\n   constant. More robustly, a release check should extract the shipped constant\n   and compare it with the manifest version.\n5. Commit source, manifest, and generated files together; tag that commit; then\n   verify the tag object/commit SHA independently on every mirror.\n\nThat shipped-bundle check would have caught the phantom update before any push.\nThe mirror SHA check remains valuable, but it is a distribution-integrity check,\nnot a build-content check.\n\n## 2. Client plane versus host plane\n\nThe two halves have different lifecycles:\n\n| Plane | Evidence/code | Load point | Effect of the v0.3.8 update |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Client | shipped `lib/client.js`, including the render toggle | The plugin frontend fetches/reloads the client bundle in the browser | The browser refreshed, so the new toggle appeared and was clickable |\n| Host | `lib/index.js`, including `CONTENT_TYPES` and the asset route registration | The running host loads the host module at boot and keeps the route handler/whitelist in memory | No host restart occurred, so the old route behavior remained active |\n\nThe probes isolate the failure to the running host process. The PNG request gets\n`200 image/png`, proving that the route is registered and alive. The SVG request\ngets the route's `404 \"unsupported image type\"`, which is an extension-whitelist\ndecision rather than a missing route. At the same time, the disk copy of the\nv0.3.8 `lib/index.js` contains `svg: 'image/svg+xml'`. Therefore the file on disk\nand the code in the release are ahead of the code already loaded by the host.\n\nA host-plane change becomes effective only after the host process is restarted,\nor after an explicit host/plugin reload mechanism that unloads the old module and\nruns route registration again. A client hot refresh changes browser code; it does\nnot mutate JavaScript objects, route tables, or module state in a separately\nrunning host. This is the exception to the usual “plugins hot-update” expectation:\nthat rule covers the client plane here, while route registration is boot-time host\nstate and needs a host restart/reload.\n\n## 3. Broken-image attribution\n\nThe source and payload disagree in a directly testable way. The source's line 232\nends with a normal `stroke-width=\"6.5\"`; the stored payload has that line ending\nin the partial attribute text `stro`, followed by line 247's arc tail beginning\n`0,1 821,730`. The real source lines 233–247 are absent. This produces the parser\nerror at the corrupted `stro0` attribute. The source passes `System.Xml`\nwell-formedness, and a later read is clean.\n\nThe corruption therefore occurred while the read-result text was being assembled\nand persisted in the session-log/result layer upstream of the plugin. It did not\noccur in the SVG file, in the host asset route, or in the plugin's renderer. The\nread tool's separate TYPE result being clean is consistent with a corruption of\nthe model-visible text block rather than the underlying file-read operation.\n\nThe plugin must treat session result text as untrusted input: a successful tool\ncall or a clean type field is not proof that the returned text is intact. It must\nvalidate the SVG before creating a render source and surface the validation error\nwith enough context to diagnose the result pipeline.\n\nEditing or “repairing” the traced file would have been the wrong response. The\nfile on disk is already well-formed, and the missing source lines cannot be safely\nrecovered by modifying the canonical asset based on a corrupted observation.\nThat would risk damaging good user data and would conceal the upstream\nnon-deterministic text-corruption bug. The correct action is to use a clean\nsource/read and report the result-text assembly defect upstream.\n\n## 4. Defensive SVG render chain\n\nUse the following ordered sources. A failed level should fall through with a\ndiagnostic reason; it should not leave a broken `<img>` as the apparent result.\n\n1. **Disk-bytes asset route.** Request the host asset route for the original path\n   and, on a successful response with the expected SVG content type, render the\n   response bytes (for example through a Blob/object URL). This is canonical,\n   avoids a text round-trip through the session log, preserves the exact file,\n   and allows the host to apply its path and content-type policy. In this\n   incident it will fail until the stale host is restarted, which is an expected\n   diagnosable fallback condition.\n2. **Validated session payload.** Only if the route is unavailable, and only if a\n   session payload is present, parse it as SVG XML before rendering it. In a\n   browser this can be:\n\n   ```js\n   const doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(payload, \"image/svg+xml\");\n   const invalid = doc.querySelector(\"parsererror\");\n   if (invalid || doc.documentElement?.localName !== \"svg\") {\n     throw new Error(\"session SVG is not well-formed\");\n   }\n   ```\n\n   The exact DOMParser error handling should also account for the target browser,\n   but the essential gate is rejecting a `parsererror` (and a non-`svg` root).\n   Only the validated text/bytes may then be used for an image/object URL. The\n   incident payload fails this gate at `stro0`, so it must not be handed to the\n   image decoder.\n3. **Sandboxed iframe as the last render fallback.** For a valid SVG that cannot\n   be displayed through the normal image path, load it in an iframe with a\n   restrictive sandbox, such as `sandbox=\"\"`, without `allow-scripts` or\n   `allow-same-origin` (and apply an appropriate restrictive CSP). The sandbox\n   blocks SVG script execution while declarative SMIL animations can still run.\n   This gives animated SVGs a document context without granting the untrusted\n   document the plugin page's script or origin privileges. A malformed session\n   payload does not bypass the XML gate merely because an iframe is available.\n4. **Explicit error state.** If the asset route fails, the payload is absent or\n   fails XML validation, and no safe fallback renders, show a deliberate error\n   state such as “SVG preview unavailable: asset route unavailable and session\n   payload is malformed,” with retry/re-read diagnostics. Remove or replace the\n   broken image element so the user is not given a silent generic broken-image\n   icon.\n\nThe order prioritizes the authoritative byte source, permits the session source\nonly when its syntax proves intact, limits the capabilities of the document-level\nfallback, and makes data loss visible instead of silently displaying corrupted\ncontent.\n\n## 5. Forensics and prevention\n\n### Decoding the concatenated-Zstandard log\n\nThe raw generation file should be copied to a separate temporary working path and\nhashed first; the evidence directory remains read-only. Decode it as a sequence of\nZstandard frames, preserving bytes and boundaries:\n\n1. Open the file in binary mode and initialize a Zstandard streaming decompressor.\n   A frame starts with the Zstandard magic bytes `28 b5 2f fd`, but magic-byte\n   searching alone must not be used to split frames because those bytes can occur\n   inside compressed data.\n2. Give the decoder the remaining input and retain both its output bytes and the\n   input position it reports as consumed. Continue until it reports end-of-frame\n   (`ret == 0` in the libzstd streaming API). Record the input offset/length,\n   output offset/length, and hashes for that frame.\n3. Advance by the decoder-reported consumed count and repeat for the next frame\n   until EOF. If using a language binding, use its one-frame/`unused_data` or\n   `bytes_consumed` facility; do not rely only on a CLI invocation that silently\n   concatenates all decompressed output and loses frame provenance.\n4. Concatenate the decompressed bytes in frame order, then parse the resulting\n   log records. Preserve UTF-8 bytes, line endings, and whitespace while extracting\n   the read-result text. Save the raw frame map and hashes alongside the decoded\n   excerpt so another investigator can reproduce the exact stored value.\n5. Compare the stored result with an independent fresh read only as an attribution\n   check. Do not fill missing lines from that fresh read when reporting what the\n   session log actually stored. In this case the exact stored payload includes the\n   `stro` + line-247-tail splice and is not well-formed XML; the clean re-read is\n   separate evidence that the source was not damaged.\n\nThe fixture provides the decoded excerpt, not the raw generation file, so the\nprocedure above is the reproducible frame-level method for the underlying artifact\nrather than a mutation of the evidence pack.\n\n### Checklist additions\n\n- Bump the version before building; make the manifest, source, and generated\n  artifacts part of the same reviewable change.\n- Grep or programmatically inspect the shipped bundle for the baked version and\n  fail the release if it differs from the manifest/tag target.\n- Verify the tag's commit/object SHA on every mirror, retaining the per-mirror\n  evidence. Keep this as a distribution check in addition to the bundle-content\n  check.\n- After host-plane changes, record a host restart/reload and probe a changed route\n  or content type from the running process; a client refresh alone is insufficient.\n- Treat session-log read-result text as untrusted: perform XML well-formedness\n  validation before SVG rendering and retain the original failure diagnostics.\n- Report the upstream session/result-text corruption bug with the exact frame,\n  offsets/record, malformed splice, and clean re-read comparison. Do not paper it\n  over by editing or “repairing” a valid traced file in the plugin.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
